WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
A property manager asks for aerial photographs of a roof after a storm.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:11.560
The job sounds simple: lift off from the parking lot, orbit the building, and send a few images before lunch.

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.420
A competent remote pilot pauses before opening the case.

4
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.040
The first decision is not which camera setting to use.

5
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:26.580
The first decision is which operating framework applies, because the purpose and circumstances of the flight determine the rules that govern it.

6
00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:40.460
Under FAA Part 107, a small unmanned aircraft system is the unmanned aircraft plus the control station, communication links, and other elements needed to operate safely in the National Airspace System.

7
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:47.440
The aircraft must weigh less than 55 pounds at takeoff, including everything onboard or otherwise attached.

8
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:55.700
A camera, light, sensor, delivery fixture, parachute system, or Remote ID broadcast module is part of that takeoff weight.

9
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.400
The rule is not limited to the casual phrase commercial drone license.

10
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:15.900
Part 107 applies to many civil operations that are not flown solely under the limited recreational exception, including business, inspection, documentation, mapping, public-safety, education, and many compensated or non-recreational flights.

11
00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:25.220
A recreational flight is governed by a different framework when it is flown strictly for recreational purposes and satisfies the statutory conditions for recreational operations.

12
00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:34.200
A roof inspection for a client, an internal facilities survey, or photographs for an organization are usually not the same thing as flying for personal enjoyment.

13
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:38.940
The pilot should not decide the framework based only on whether money changes hands.

14
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:45.440
The mission purpose, the organization benefiting from the flight, and the planned use of the data all matter.

15
00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:54.360
To act as remote pilot in command under Part 107, a person needs a Remote Pilot Certificate with a small UAS rating and current aeronautical knowledge.

16
00:01:54.460 --> 00:02:17.980
The basic eligibility path requires the applicant to be at least 16, be able to read, speak, write, and understand English, be physically and mentally able to operate safely, obtain an FAA Tracking Number through IACRA, schedule and pass the Unmanned Aircraft General - Small knowledge test, and then complete the certificate application process.

17
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:26.000
Certain current Part 61 pilot certificate holders with a flight review may use the FAA online training path instead of the initial knowledge test.

18
00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:39.940
Certificate holders must maintain aeronautical knowledge recency within the previous 24 calendar months, typically through the appropriate online training, and the certificate and identification must be physically possessed and readily accessible during operations.

19
00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:43.580
Part 107 also separates roles.

20
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.120
The remote pilot in command is directly responsible for the operation and is the final authority.

21
00:02:49.220 --> 00:03:02.740
A person manipulating the controls may fly only if that person is certificated and current or is under the direct supervision of the remote pilot in command, with the remote pilot able to immediately take direct control.

22
00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:12.440
A visual observer can help see and avoid traffic or hazards, but the observer does not transfer responsibility away from the remote pilot in command.

23
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:22.000
The roof mission may require aircraft registration, Remote ID compliance, airspace authorization, property access permission, insurance review, and a site safety plan.

24
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.820
None of those items substitutes for the others.

25
00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:29.000
Certification grants authority to operate within a regulatory framework.

26
00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:39.020
It does not prove that this aircraft is fit, this site is clear, this weather is acceptable, this client pressure is reasonable, or this pilot has enough practical skill for the task.

27
00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:49.440
The operational takeaway is simple: before launching, identify the governing framework, confirm each separate requirement, and then decide whether the mission can be flown safely.
